Let $q>1$ be a non-integer real number, set $\theta = 1/q$, $k = \lfloor q\rfloor$, and $A_k = \{0,1,\dots,k\}$. For $x\in[0,1)$ let $\varepsilon_1(x),\varepsilon_2(x),\dots \in A_k$ denote the digits of the regular (greedy) Rényi–Parry $q$-expansion of $x$, so that $x = \sum_{n\ge 1}\varepsilon_n(x)\theta^n$.

A function $F:[0,1)\to\mathbb{C}$ is called additive (with respect to the Rényi–Parry expansion) if
\begin{enumerate}
\item $F(0)=0$;
\item $\sum_{l\ge 1}\sum_{a=1}^{k} |F(a\theta^l)| < \infty$;
\item for every $x\in[0,1)$, $F(x) = \sum_{n\ge 1} F(\varepsilon_n(x)\theta^n)$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $t = t_1 t_2 t_3 \cdots$ be the quasi-regular expansion of $1$ in base $q$, and define
$$G(z) = 1 - t_1 z - t_2 z^2 - t_3 z^3 - \cdots.$$
For each root $\rho$ of $G(z)=0$ with $|\rho|<1$ of multiplicity $m$, and each integer $j$ with $0\le j \le m-1$, consider the sequence $u_\nu = \nu^{j}\rho^{\nu}$. The continuous additive functions arising from such sequences, together with the closed linear span (in the uniform topology on continuous additive functions) of all such functions, are called the elementary continuous additive functions.

Question: Is every continuous additive function $F:[0,1)\to\mathbb{C}$ elementary?

